{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED on exact head 70f986d3ad3d84c6077ad06a2422b11919ce72a4, on one narrow finding. All three of my earlier findings are genuinely closed and I verified each by execution rather than by reading.\nCLOSED, measured. I re-ran the probe that exposed the gap last time — actor 'other' filing a report resting on agent's effective promise — and the preflight now refuses it: 'report actor must be the promisor of its promise-lifecycle basis'. Two effective promises in rests_on is refused: 'report requires exactly one effective promise-lifecycle basis in rests_on; got 2'. The valid case still lands. Lifecycle is read from the active vocabulary rather than the kind name, and validateReportBasis re-derives it from the snapshot's Definitions, so a declared custom promise kind participates and a workroom that redefined report would not be forced through the check.\nFINDING, blocking, and reproduced: the cachedLifecycle fast path opens a hole for exactly the custom kinds this change added support for. BuildActRequest enters the check only when act.Kind == KindReport or cachedLifecycle(act.Kind) == LifecycleReport, and cachedLifecycle returns LifecycleNone whenever w.snapshotCache is nil. A process that has not yet taken a snapshot therefore cannot recognise a custom report-lifecycle kind, and skips the preflight entirely. I built your TestReportPreflightUsesDeclaredLifecycleKinds fixture, then opened a second Workspace on the same repository — which is what a fresh gs invocation does — and filed a 'delivery' report against another actor's promise as that process's first act. It was admitted. The fold then refuses it, which is precisely the landed-with-no-force outcome this whole change exists to prevent, reached through the door the change opened.\nTHE FIX IS A DELETION, which is why I am confident it is worth blocking for. validateReportBasis already re-checks the lifecycle from the authoritative snapshot and returns nil when the kind is not a report lifecycle. The cachedLifecycle gate can therefore only ever produce a false negative: it cannot admit anything the inner check would reject, and it can skip work the inner check would do. Dropping it and calling validateReportBasis for every state act makes the behaviour correct with no loss of meaning, at the cost of one snapshot on non-report state acts — and Snapshot is cached, which is the same argument I accepted from you last round for the cost being acceptable.\nIf you would rather keep a fast path, it needs to fail open into the check rather than out of it: unknown lifecycle should mean 'ask the snapshot', not 'skip'.\nGATES at the exact head: diff --check clean, vet ok, build ok, internal/app 27.0s green, full go test green, internal/docset ok at 82.2s uncached.\nARCHITECTURE: unchanged and correct — the preflight mirrors the fold and the fold stays authoritative; nothing moves into the kernel. SECURITY: no boundary moves; the residual is availability of a check, not authority. SIMPLIFICATION: the finding above is the simplification — the fast path is redundant with the check it guards.\nFiling note: 6e1b7caf at internal/app and 3b454154 at docs both name this exact head, so the merge blocker from last round does not recur.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6e1b7caf4d692b0957954a38e25adf5fe0403c37","head":"70f986d3ad3d84c6077ad06a2422b11919ce72a4","verdict":"changes-requested"}}